  </Steps>
  <p>A <Term id="tsr" /> such as ARABART is a program that ends but leaves itself in memory. The <Term id="install-check">install check</Term> reads the code behind the current INT 10h and looks for his name tag, <code>DALYASSER</code>. If it is there, the program prints "This utility is already active in your RAM." and exits without loading again.</p>
  <p>Then it calls <Term id="int-27h" />, the oldest "stay resident" call in DOS. It never unloads; to remove it, you restart the PC. That was normal for small resident programs of the time.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'A TSR that hooks INT 10h', now: 'A background service, or a shared library that every app loads', why: 'Loaded once, then any program can call it.' },
      { then: 'The "DALYASSER" check before loading', now: 'A single-instance lock (a lock file or a named mutex)', why: 'It refuses to run a second copy.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the install code, byte by byte">
    <Listing
      title="ARABART.EXE · install (CS:0000) and exit (CS:74D4)"
      lines={[
        { off: '0000', code: 'mov  ax,3510h', note: 'DOS: who answers INT 10h? -> ES:BX' },
        { off: '0003', code: 'int  21h' },
        { off: '0007', code: 'mov  si,003Ch', note: 'my own handler entry' },
        { off: '000C', code: 'mov  cx,12' },
        { off: '0010', code: 'repe cmpsb', note: 'same 12 bytes? then I am already loaded', hi: true },
        { off: '0012', code: 'jne  0027h' },
        { off: '0027', code: 'mov  [0051h],es', note: 'old vector goes into my JMP FAR operand' },
        { off: '002B', code: 'mov  [004Fh],bx' },
        { off: '002F', code: 'mov  ax,2510h / int 21h', note: 'INT 10h now points at CS:003C' },
        { off: '74E3', code: 'mov  cx,112', note: 'wait 112 vertical retraces, port 3DAh bit 3' },
        { off: '74F9', code: 'mov  dx,759Eh', note: 'bytes to keep' },
        { off: '74FC', code: 'int  27h', note: 'terminate and stay resident', hi: true },
      ]}
      foot="Offsets are CS offsets in the unpacked image; the unpacked file adds a 30h-byte header in front."
    />
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Resident size:</strong> MS-DOS 2.0's source (<code>PROC.ASM</code>, <code>Stay_resident</code>) shows INT 27h counts DX from the <Term id="psp" />, not from the code segment. His DX is the image size counted from CS, so DOS keeps 256 bytes fewer than the image. Those bytes are only the exit code and messages, so nothing breaks.</li>
      <li><strong>Weak spot:</strong> the check reads only the code behind the current INT 10h. If another program hooks INT 10h after ARABART, a second run loads a second 30 KB copy.</li>
      <li>The 1.6-second banner pause counts 112 <Term id="vertical-retrace">vertical retraces</Term> on port 3DAh, a timer that does not depend on CPU speed.</li>
      <li>Writing the old vector into its own <code>JMP FAR</code> is <Term id="self-modifying-code" />, a common TSR idiom.</li>
      <li>The environment block is not freed, and there is no INT 2Fh multiplex and no uninstall call. See the bytes: <a href={at(0x3c)}>handler entry 003Ch</a>, <a href={at(0x74d4)}>exit code 74D4h</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="api">
  <h2>How do other programs ask it to draw?</h2>
  <p class="lead">They call the normal video interrupt with <code>AH = 36h</code>. AL picks one of 11 functions, and four registers carry the position, the color and the text.</p>
  <Figure title="One call: draw Arabic at x = 630, y = 18" caption="The call DEMO.EXE makes for its first line. ARABART's handler checks AH; everything that is not 36h goes on to the video BIOS unchanged.">
