  <p>This is what made ARABART easy. A programmer did not link a library or learn a new file format. They loaded four registers, as they already did for every DOS and <Term id="bios" /> service, and called <code>int 10h</code>. That is a <Term id="register-convention">register calling convention</Term>, and any language that could call an interrupt could use it.</p>
  <h3>The keyboard is the alphabet</h3>
  <p>The text itself is plain ASCII. Each English key stands for the Arabic letter printed on the same key of an Arabic keyboard, so <code>~gtk ~guvfd</code> means الفن العربي. We call this the <Term id="key-encoding">Arabic-keyboard encoding</Term>. A 96-byte table at 0682h turns each key into a <Term id="glyph" /> code. The top two bits of that code already say how the letter connects.</p>
  <Figure title="The table at 0682h, unshifted keys" caption="Each key shows the Arabic letter and the code from his table. Blue keys can join on both sides (code C0h to FFh). White keys join only the letter before them (40h to 7Fh). The dashed key joins nothing (00h to 3Fh).">
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-kb-t">
      <title id="ab-kb-t">A QWERTY keyboard where each key shows its Arabic letter and ARABART's code, colored by how the letter joins.</title>
      {KB.map((row: (string | number)[][], r: number) => row.map((k, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class={keyClass(k[3] as number)} x={10 + r * 24 + i * 48} y={10 + r * 82} width="44" height="74" />
          <text class:list={['t-cy', keyText(k[3] as number)]} x={16 + r * 24 + i * 48} y={28 + r * 82}>{k[0]}</text>
          <text class:list={['t-ar', 't-lg', keyText(k[3] as number)]} x={32 + r * 24 + i * 48} y={56 + r * 82} text-anchor="middle">{k[1]}</text>
          <text class={keyText(k[3] as number)} x={32 + r * 24 + i * 48} y={78 + r * 82} text-anchor="middle">{k[2]}</text>
        </g>
      )))}
      <rect class="box" x="10" y="264" width="24" height="24" /><text x="42" y="282">joins both sides</text>
      <rect class="box-ink" x="220" y="264" width="24" height="24" /><text x="252" y="282">joins right only</text>
      <rect class="box-dash" x="430" y="264" width="24" height="24" /><text x="462" y="282">joins none</text>
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 360 560" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-kb-tn">
      <title id="ab-kb-tn">The keyboard table, six rows of keys with their Arabic letters and codes.</title>
      {NARROW_KB.map((row: (string | number)[][], r: number) => row.map((k, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class={keyClass(k[3] as number)} x={6 + (r % 2) * 25 + i * 50} y={6 + r * 82} width="46" height="74" />
          <text class:list={['t-cy', keyText(k[3] as number)]} x={12 + (r % 2) * 25 + i * 50} y={24 + r * 82}>{k[0]}</text>
          <text class:list={['t-ar', 't-lg', keyText(k[3] as number)]} x={29 + (r % 2) * 25 + i * 50} y={52 + r * 82} text-anchor="middle">{k[1]}</text>
          <text class={keyText(k[3] as number)} x={29 + (r % 2) * 25 + i * 50} y={74 + r * 82} text-anchor="middle">{k[2]}</text>
        </g>
      )))}
      <rect class="box" x="6" y="502" width="22" height="22" /><text x="34" y="519">both</text>
      <rect class="box-ink" x="110" y="502" width="22" height="22" /><text x="138" y="519">right only</text>
      <rect class="box-dash" x="262" y="502" width="22" height="22" /><text x="290" y="519">none</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Styles are typed into the same string with the shifted number keys: <code>!</code> italic, <code>@</code> bold, <code>#</code> underline, <code>$</code> double size, <code>*</code> reset. The table turns them into codes C9h, C8h, CAh, CBh and D0h. So a whole formatted line is one ordinary C string, for example <code>" #~gtk ~guvfd 51&lt;4#"</code> in DEMO.EXE.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'INT 10h with AH = 36h, AL = function', now: 'A system call number, or a Unix <code>ioctl</code> request code', why: 'One entry point; a number chooses the service.' },
      { then: 'Arguments in AX, BX, CX and DX', now: 'System call arguments in registers', why: 'The cheapest way to hand values to code you did not link with.' },
      { then: 'Arabic stored as the English keys of the Arabic layout', now: 'UTF-8 text typed through an operating-system keyboard layout', why: 'In both, the keyboard layout maps keys to letters. Today the stored text is the letter itself.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the full API and the C binding">
    <div class="ab-tbl">
      <table>
        <caption>INT 10h, AH = 36h</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>AX</th><th>Function</th><th>In</th><th>Out</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>3600h</td><td>Install check</td><td>none</td><td>AX = 5944h ("YD"), BX = 040Fh (v4.15), CX = 4</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3601h</td><td>Set colors</td><td>CL text, CH shadow</td><td>none</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3602h</td><td>Get colors</td><td>none</td><td>CL, CH</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3603h</td><td>Select font</td><td>CX = 1 to 5</td><td>bad number: carry set, CX = 5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3604h</td><td>Font info</td><td>none</td><td>CX = font, DX = line height</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3605h</td><td>Measure</td><td>DS:DX text</td><td>CX = width in pixels</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3606h</td><td>Measure 3-D</td><td>DS:DX text</td><td>CX = width × 2 (bug 3)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3607h</td><td>Draw</td><td>DS:DX text, CX right edge, BX line bottom (FFFFh = keep cursor)</td><td>CX, DX = new pen; AL = 0 ok, 1 bad mode, 2 off screen</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3608h</td><td>Draw 3-D</td><td>as 3607h</td><td>as 3607h</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3609h</td><td>Set cursor</td><td>CX = x, BX = y</td><td>none</td></tr>
          <tr><td>360Ah</td><td>Get cursor</td><td>none</td><td>CX = x, BX = y</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Listing
      title="DEMO.EXE · the C binding (image 2083h, 212Ch)"
      lines={[
        { off: '2079', code: 'push ax / push bx / push cx / push dx', note: 'save everything the driver may change' },
        { off: '207D', code: 'mov  ax,3601h', note: 'set colors' },
        { off: '2080', code: 'mov  cx,[bp+6]', note: 'the C argument' },
        { off: '2083', code: 'int  10h', hi: true },
        { off: '2141', code: 'call text_height', note: 'y + height: from "top" to ARABART\'s "line bottom"' },
        { off: '2146', code: 'add  [bp+8],ax' },
        { off: '214E', code: 'mov  cx,[bp+6] / mov bx,[bp+8]' },
        { off: '2154', code: 'lds  dx,[bp+0Ah]', note: 'far pointer to the string' },
        { off: '2157', code: 'mov  ax,3607h / int 10h', note: 'draw', hi: true },
      ]}
      lang="asm"
      foot="The binding is Borland C++ with inline assembly; the function names are ours. The same binding is compiled into SUN.EXE."
    />
    <p><strong>Register contract:</strong> functions 4 to 8 change BX, and functions 5 to 8 return garbage in AH. On a bad font number, AX, BX and DX come back holding the caller's return address and flags. Only a bad video mode sets the <Term id="carry-flag">carry flag</Term>; "off screen" returns AL = 2 with carry clear. His C binding avoids all of this by saving every register around each call.</p>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="shaping">
  <h2>How does it choose the shape of each letter?</h2>
  <p class="lead">Every letter code carries two "hands": bit 7 reaches the next letter and bit 6 the previous one. One pass drops each hand that has no partner, and the bits that remain are the letter's form.</p>
  <Steps
    title="Shaping الفن, typed as ~gtk"
    steps={[
      'He types <code>~gtk</code>: the keys under ا ل ف ن. Arabic runs right to left, so the first key is drawn at the right.',
      'The table at 0682h gives each key a code. Bit 7 is the left hand (it can join the next letter); bit 6 is the right hand (it can join the previous letter).',
      'The first letter has nothing before it, so it loses bit 6. Alef (30h) has no hands at all, so it stays 30h.',
      'Alef cannot reach left, so lam loses its right hand: E9h becomes A9h.',
      'Lam and feh both reach, and so do feh and noon: those pairs stay joined.',
      'Noon is last, so it loses its left hand: EDh becomes 6Dh.',
      'The final codes index the font: isolated alef, initial lam, medial feh, final noon. Drawn right to left, they join.',
    ]}
  >
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 520" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-sh-t">
      <title id="ab-sh-t">The keys ~ g t k become codes 30, E9, F6, ED, the shaper checks each pair of neighbors, and the result 30, A9, F6, 6D draws الفن.</title>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="46">key</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="116">table</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="182">check</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="242">shaped</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="392">glyph</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="560" y="492">joined</text>
      {WALK.map((w, i) => (
        <g>
          <g data-s="1+">
            <rect class="box" x={WX[i] - 40} y="20" width="80" height="40" />
            <text x={WX[i] - 18} y="46" text-anchor="middle">{w.key}</text>
            <text class="t-ar t-lg" x={WX[i] + 16} y="48" text-anchor="middle">{w.ar}</text>
          </g>
          <g data-s="2+">
            <rect class="box" x={WX[i] - 30} y="88" width="60" height="44" />
            <text x={WX[i]} y="116" text-anchor="middle">{w.table}</text>
            <rect class={w.tb[0] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x={WX[i] - 52} y="102" width="16" height="16" />
            <rect class={w.tb[1] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x={WX[i] + 36} y="102" width="16" height="16" />
          </g>
          <g data-s={`${shapedStep[i]}+`}>
            <rect class="box-hi" x={WX[i] - 30} y="214" width="60" height="44" />
            <text class="t-bk" x={WX[i]} y="242" text-anchor="middle">{w.code}</text>
            <rect class={w.sb[0] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x={WX[i] - 52} y="228" width="16" height="16" />
            <rect class={w.sb[1] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x={WX[i] + 36} y="228" width="16" height="16" />
            <text x={WX[i]} y="282" text-anchor="middle">{w.form}</text>
          </g>
          <g data-s="7">
            {tri(DATA.walk[w.g].r).map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={WX[i] + DATA.walk[w.g].adv + x * 2} y={400 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
          </g>
        </g>
      ))}
      <text class="t-yl" x="515" y="182" text-anchor="middle" data-s="3+">break</text>
      <text class="t-yl" x="390" y="182" text-anchor="middle" data-s="4+">break</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="270" y="182" text-anchor="middle" data-s="5+">join</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="150" y="182" text-anchor="middle" data-s="5+">join</text>
      <text class="t-yl" x="32" y="182" text-anchor="middle" data-s="6+">break</text>
      <g data-s="7">
        {word.runs.map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={320 + word.width + x * 2} y={500 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
      </g>
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 360 540" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-sh-tn">
      <title id="ab-sh-tn">Each of the four keys becomes a table code, then a shaped code and a glyph; the four glyphs join into الفن.</title>
      <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="16">key</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="92" y="16">table</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="190" y="16">shaped</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="296" y="16">glyph</text>
      <text class="t-yl" x="140" y="36" text-anchor="middle" data-s="3+">start: break</text>
      {WALK.map((w, i) => (
        <g>
          <g data-s="1+">
            <rect class="box" x="6" y={44 + i * 96} width="58" height="44" />
            <text x="16" y={72 + i * 96}>{w.key}</text>
            <text class="t-ar t-lg" x="48" y={74 + i * 96} text-anchor="middle">{w.ar}</text>
          </g>
          <g data-s="2+">
            <rect class="box" x="92" y={44 + i * 96} width="48" height="44" />
            <text x="116" y={72 + i * 96} text-anchor="middle">{w.table}</text>
            <rect class={w.tb[0] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x="74" y={58 + i * 96} width="14" height="14" />
            <rect class={w.tb[1] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x="144" y={58 + i * 96} width="14" height="14" />
          </g>
          <g data-s={`${shapedStep[i]}+`}>
            <rect class="box-hi" x="190" y={44 + i * 96} width="48" height="44" />
            <text class="t-bk" x="214" y={72 + i * 96} text-anchor="middle">{w.code}</text>
            <rect class={w.sb[0] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x="172" y={58 + i * 96} width="14" height="14" />
            <rect class={w.sb[1] ? 'box-hi' : 'box-dash'} x="242" y={58 + i * 96} width="14" height="14" />
            <text x="214" y={108 + i * 96} text-anchor="middle">{w.form}</text>
          </g>
          <g data-s="7">
            {tri(DATA.walk[w.g].r).map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={350 + x} y={100 + i * 96 + y} width={n} height="1" />)}
          </g>
        </g>
      ))}
      <text class="t-yl" x="116" y="132" text-anchor="middle" data-s="4+">break</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="116" y="228" text-anchor="middle" data-s="5+">join</text>
      <text class="t-cy" x="116" y="324" text-anchor="middle" data-s="5+">join</text>
      <text class="t-yl" x="116" y="420" text-anchor="middle" data-s="6+">end: break</text>
      <g data-s="7">
        {word.runs.map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={180 + word.width + x * 2} y={530 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
      </g>
    </svg>
  </Steps>
  <p>This is <Term id="auto-shaping" />, and the trick is that the shape is not stored anywhere else. The 256 slots of each font are 64 base codes times 4 forms: code + 00h is isolated, + 40h final, + 80h initial, + C0h medial. The table stores every letter at its "most joining" form, so the shaper only ever clears bits. It never has to know which letter it is looking at.</p>
  <p>Two details show care. The style codes C8h to CBh and D0h sit in slots that digits would use for their medial form, and digits never join, so no letter can ever turn into a style code. The shaper also skips style codes when it looks for a neighbor, so <code>~gt@k</code> still joins ف to ن across the bold switch.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'Bits 7 and 6 of every glyph code', now: 'The Unicode <code>Joining_Type</code> of every letter (D, R, U)', why: 'Both say which neighbors a letter can join.' },
      { then: 'Style codes the shaper steps over', now: 'Joining_Type T (transparent), for example vowel marks', why: 'They sit between letters without breaking the join.' },
      { then: 'Code + 00h, 40h, 80h, C0h for the 4 forms', now: 'The OpenType features <code>isol</code>, <code>fina</code>, <code>init</code>, <code>medi</code>', why: 'HarfBuzz picks the same 4 forms, then asks the font for each glyph.' },
      { then: 'لا has its own key (<code>b</code>)', now: 'A required ligature (<code>rlig</code>) inside the font', why: 'Today the engine builds لا from ل and ا by itself.' },
      { then: 'Digits typed in reverse (<code>51&lt;4</code> shows ٤,١٥)', now: 'The Unicode Bidirectional Algorithm', why: 'Today numbers inside Arabic are reordered automatically.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the shaping loop and what it cannot do">
    <Listing
      title="ARABART.EXE · shape (CS:0299)"
      lines={[
        { off: '0299', code: 'mov  ch,cl', note: 'CL = next letter, style codes already skipped' },
        { off: '029B', code: 'mov  dh,dl', note: 'DL = this letter' },
        { off: '029D', code: 'and  dh,80h', note: 'can this one reach left?' },
        { off: '02A0', code: 'and  ch,40h', note: 'can the next one reach right?' },
        { off: '02A3', code: 'cmp  dh,0 / je 02B0' },
        { off: '02A8', code: 'cmp  ch,0 / je 02B0' },
        { off: '02AD', code: 'jmp  02BA', note: 'both reach: stay joined' },
        { off: '02B0', code: 'and  dl,7Fh', note: 'otherwise this letter lets go on the left', hi: true },
        { off: '02B3', code: 'and  cl,0BFh', note: 'and the next lets go on the right', hi: true },
        { off: '02B6', code: 'mov  [di],dl / mov [si],cl' },
      ]}
      foot="The whole pass, including the style-code skipping, runs from 022F to 02BB."
    />
    <p><a href={at(0x22f)}>Open the shaping pass in the byte map</a>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No automatic لا:</strong> typing <code>g</code> then <code>h</code> draws لـ + ـا, two letters, not the <Term id="ligature" />. The writer must press <code>b</code> (لا), <code>G</code> (لأ) or <code>B</code> (لآ), as on a typewriter.</li>
      <li><strong>No <Term id="bidi" />:</strong> X is the right edge and every glyph moves the pen left. Numbers must be stored reversed. There are no Latin letters in the fonts at all.</li>
      <li><strong>No <Term id="harakat" />:</strong> the vowel keys of the Arabic layout map to nothing. <Term id="kashida" /> (<code>J</code>) is supported and joins on both sides.</li>
      <li><strong>Egyptian conventions:</strong> final and isolated yeh are drawn without dots, so <code>d</code> (ي) and <code>n</code> (ى) share one code, and digits are <Term id="arabic-indic-digits" />.</li>
    </ul>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="glyphs">
  <h2>How is one letter stored?</h2>
  <p class="lead">As a list of horizontal runs: "skip this many pixels from the right edge, paint this many". Byte EFh moves up one row and FFh ends the letter.</p>
  <Figure title="Font 1, code 37h: the letter ع" caption="The pointer table of font 1 starts at 0706h. Entry 37h sits at 0774h and points to 0F38h, where these 34 bytes are. Each line of bytes paints the grid row beside it, starting at the bottom.">
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-gl-t" data-runs>
      <title id="ab-gl-t">The 34 bytes of the glyph ع and the 10 rows of pixel runs they paint, from the bottom up and right to left.</title>
      <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="20">0774h: 0F38h</text>
      <text x="160" y="20">-&gt; glyph bytes at 0F38h</text>
      {Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, r) => Array.from({ length: 8 }, (_, c) => (
        <rect class="box-bk" x={40 + c * 20} y={70 + r * 20} width="20" height="20" />
      )))}
      {FMT.map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px run" x={40 + (x + 7) * 20} y={290 + y * 20} width={n * 20} height="20" />)}
      <path class="wire-hi" d="M30 310 H210" />
      <text class="t-yl" x="30" y="326">line bottom (y)</text>
      <path class="wire dash" d="M200 50 V318" />
      <text class="t-cy" x="206" y="56">right edge (x)</text>
      {FMT_BYTES.map(l => (
        <g>
          <text x="250" y={305 + l.r * 20}>{l.b}</text>
          {l.n && <text class="t-cy" x="350" y={305 + l.r * 20}>{l.n}</text>}
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 360 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-gl-tn" data-runs>
      <title id="ab-gl-tn">The glyph ع as bytes and as the pixel runs they paint.</title>
      <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="20">0774h -&gt; 0F38h</text>
      {Array.from({ length: 12 }, (_, r) => Array.from({ length: 8 }, (_, c) => (
        <rect class="box-bk" x={20 + c * 18} y={70 + r * 18} width="18" height="18" />
      )))}
      {FMT.map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px run" x={20 + (x + 7) * 18} y={268 + y * 18} width={n * 18} height="18" />)}
      <path class="wire-hi" d="M10 286 H170" />
      <text class="t-yl" x="10" y="308">line bottom (y)</text>
      {FMT_BYTES.map(l => <text x="190" y={282 + l.r * 18}>{l.b}</text>)}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>The first byte is the letter's width (8) and the second is how many rows above the line bottom it starts (2). This <Term id="run-length-encoding">run-length</Term> layout is <strong>not</strong> smaller than a plain bitmap: we measured it at 1.16 to 2.23 times larger. He likely chose it because runs make styles nearly free, and because the drawing loop never has to test a blank pixel.</p>
  <h3>Five fonts, one of them for a single word</h3>
  <Figure title="The 5 fonts inside ARABART.EXE" caption="Each sample is drawn from his bytes. Fonts 1 to 4 show الفن; font 5 has only the glyphs for الشمس (The Sun) plus a space, and borrows the other 143 slots from font 1.">
    <svg class="dg" viewBox={`0 0 640 ${FONT_H}`} role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-fo-t">
      <title id="ab-fo-t">Five font samples with their heights, glyph counts and sizes; the last font spells الشمس.</title>
      {FONT_ROWS.map(f => (
        <g>
          <text x="10" y={f.top + f.h + 6}>{f.label}</text>
          <rect class="box-bk" x={620 - f.w * 2 - 10} y={f.top} width={f.w * 2 + 20} height={f.h * 2 + 20} />
          {tri(f.r).map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={620 - f.w * 2 + x * 2} y={f.top + 10 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox={`0 0 360 ${FONT_HN}`} role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-fo-tn">
      <title id="ab-fo-tn">Five font samples from ARABART.EXE.</title>
      {FONT_ROWS_N.map(f => (
        <g>
          <text x="10" y={f.top + 14}>{f.label.split(' · ').slice(0, 3).join(' · ')}</text>
          <rect class="box-bk" x={350 - f.w * 2 - 10} y={f.top + 24} width={f.w * 2 + 20} height={f.h * 2 + 16} />
          {tri(f.r).map(([x, y, n]) => <rect class="ab-px" x={350 - f.w * 2 + x * 2} y={f.top + 32 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <p>Font 5 is 50 pixels tall and contains exactly ا, لـ, ـشـ, ـمـ and ـس. The Brave Sun (SUN.EXE, 1993) selects font 5 three times, so this font was almost certainly made for that program, likely for its title. The memory order (fonts 1, 3, 4, 2, 5) and font 4's header, copied from font 1, suggest the fonts were added one by one.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'Hand-drawn pixel runs, one set per size', now: 'Outline fonts (TrueType, OpenType) scaled to any size by a rasterizer such as FreeType', why: 'Both map a character code to a glyph shape through a table.' },
      { then: 'A 256-entry pointer table per font', now: 'The <code>cmap</code> and glyph tables inside a font file', why: 'Look up the code, follow the pointer, draw what you find.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the font header trick and the cost of runs">
    <ul>
      <li>Slots 0 to 2 of each pointer table can never hold a letter (space is code 03h), so he stores metrics there: word 1 is the line height (18, 27, 34, 38, 50) and word 2 is the 3-D shadow offset (1, 1, 2, 2, 3 pixels).</li>
      <li>Identical forms share one glyph: font 1 points 54 slots at glyphs another slot already uses.</li>
      <li>Raw bitmap vs runs, measured over unique glyphs: font 1 1,316 vs 2,930 bytes; font 3 8,884 vs 10,326 bytes.</li>
      <li>The fonts span <a href={at(0x706)}>0706h to 7421h</a> in the byte map.</li>
    </ul>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="vga">
  <h2>How does a letter reach the screen?</h2>
  <p class="lead">ARABART writes to the <Term id="vga" /> card directly. It sets the color once, then paints up to 8 pixels in all 4 color planes with a single memory write.</p>
  <Steps
    title="One run of 6 pixels, yellow, at x 128 to 133, y 100"
    steps={[
      'Ask the BIOS for the screen mode (<code>INT 10h/0Fh</code>). Mode 12h means 480 rows of 80 bytes at segment A000h; 10h and 0Eh also work.',
      'Set the color once per string: Graphics Controller index 1 (Enable Set/Reset) = 0Fh, index 0 (Set/Reset) = the color, here 0Eh.',
      'Find the byte: 100 × 80 + 128 ÷ 8 = 1F50h. His loop gets x ÷ 8 and x mod 8 together, with the bits reversed, and the table <code>00 04 02 06 01 05 03 07</code> puts them back.',
      'Build the mask: each pixel of the run ORs one bit from the table <code>80 40 20 10 08 04 02 01</code>. Six pixels give FCh.',
      'Write index 8 (Bit Mask) = FCh, read the byte to fill the latches, write it back. All 4 planes change at once, only under the mask.',
      'At the end: index 1 = 0 and index 8 = FFh, so Borland graphics keeps working normally.',
    ]}
  >
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-vga-t">
      <title id="ab-vga-t">The CPU sets the VGA Graphics Controller's Set/Reset and Bit Mask registers, then one write at A000:1F50 paints six yellow pixels into four bit planes.</title>
      <g data-s="1+">
        <text x="10" y="24">INT 10h/0Fh -&gt; mode 12h</text>
        <text x="10" y="48">480 rows × 80 bytes</text>
        <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="72">at segment A000h</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="2+">
        <rect class="box" x="10" y="100" width="240" height="124" />
        <text class="t-cy" x="22" y="124">Graphics Ctrl 3CEh</text>
        <text x="22" y="152">1 Enable S/R = 0Fh</text>
        <text x="22" y="178">0 Set/Reset  = 0Eh</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="5+">
        <text class="t-yl" x="22" y="204">8 Bit Mask   = FCh</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="3+">
        <text x="280" y="24">offset = 100×80 + 128÷8</text>
        <text class="t-yl" x="280" y="48">= 1F50h, first pixel</text>
        <text class="t-cy" x="280" y="72">00 04 02 06 01 05 03 07</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="4+">
        <text x="280" y="104">A000:1F50, x 128 to 135</text>
        {[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0].map((b, i) => (
          <g>
            <rect class={b ? 'box-hi' : 'box-bk'} x={290 + i * 32} y="114" width="30" height="30" />
            <text class={b ? 't-bk' : ''} x={305 + i * 32} y="135" text-anchor="middle">{b}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
        <text class="t-yl" x="550" y="135">= FCh</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="5+">
        {[[3, 1], [2, 1], [1, 1], [0, 0]].map(([p, v], r) => (
          <g>
            {[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0].map((m, i) => (
              <rect class={m ? (v ? 'box-hi' : 'box-ink') : 'box-bk'} x={290 + i * 32} y={176 + r * 24} width="30" height="20" />
            ))}
            <text x="550" y={192 + r * 24}>p{p} = {v}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
        <text class="t-cy" x="290" y="286">read fills the latches, write paints</text>
        <text class="t-cy" x="290" y="308">0Eh = 1110b: planes 3, 2, 1 on, 0 off</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="6">
        <text class="t-yl" x="10" y="270">done: index 1 = 0</text>
        <text class="t-yl" x="10" y="294">index 8 = FFh</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 360 560" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-vga-tn">
      <title id="ab-vga-tn">Setting Set/Reset and Bit Mask, then one write paints six pixels into four planes.</title>
      <g data-s="1+">
        <text x="10" y="22">INT 10h/0Fh -&gt; mode 12h</text>
        <text x="10" y="44">480 rows × 80 bytes, A000h</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="2+">
        <rect class="box" x="10" y="60" width="340" height="110" />
        <text class="t-cy" x="22" y="84">Graphics Ctrl 3CEh</text>
        <text x="22" y="110">1 Enable S/R = 0Fh</text>
        <text x="22" y="134">0 Set/Reset  = 0Eh</text>
      </g>
      <text class="t-yl" x="22" y="158" data-s="5+">8 Bit Mask   = FCh</text>
      <g data-s="3+">
        <text x="10" y="198">100×80 + 128÷8 = 1F50h</text>
        <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="222">00 04 02 06 01 05 03 07</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="4+">
        <text x="10" y="254">A000:1F50, x 128 to 135</text>
        {[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0].map((b, i) => (
          <g>
            <rect class={b ? 'box-hi' : 'box-bk'} x={10 + i * 34} y="264" width="32" height="30" />
            <text class={b ? 't-bk' : ''} x={26 + i * 34} y="285" text-anchor="middle">{b}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
        <text class="t-yl" x="290" y="285">FCh</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="5+">
        {[[3, 1], [2, 1], [1, 1], [0, 0]].map(([p, v], r) => (
          <g>
            {[1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0].map((m, i) => (
              <rect class={m ? (v ? 'box-hi' : 'box-ink') : 'box-bk'} x={10 + i * 34} y={314 + r * 26} width="32" height="22" />
            ))}
            <text x="290" y={331 + r * 26}>p{p}: {v}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
        <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="438">read fills latches,</text>
        <text class="t-cy" x="10" y="460">write paints all 4 planes</text>
      </g>
      <g data-s="6">
        <text class="t-yl" x="10" y="500">done: index 1 = 0,</text>
        <text class="t-yl" x="10" y="522">index 8 = FFh</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </Steps>
  <p>In 16-color VGA each pixel's color is split over 4 <Term id="bit-planes">bit planes</Term>. Writing them one by one would cost 4 writes per byte. ARABART uses the card's <Term id="graphics-controller" />: <Term id="set-reset" /> supplies the color for all planes, the <Term id="bit-mask" /> picks the pixels, and the <Term id="latches" /> keep every other pixel as it was. We counted <strong>18.5 instructions per pixel</strong> for a line in font 3 when we ran his code in an emulator. When it finishes, it resets the two registers it changed, so <Term id="bgi">Borland graphics (BGI)</Term> can keep drawing.</p>
  <p>The bit math is his own. To get x ÷ 8 and x mod 8 he shifts x right 3 times and rotates each bit into BX, which leaves the 3 low bits reversed. The table <code>00 04 02 06 01 05 03 07</code> reverses them back. A textbook would write <code>and dx,7</code>. The same 8 bytes appear in PESM and in ARAB-ART 7.15, and the same trick in WINKEY.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'Set/Reset + Bit Mask: one write paints up to 8 pixels in 4 planes', now: 'A GPU fill with a write mask', why: 'Set the color once; the hardware decides which pixels change.' },
      { then: 'Restore index 1 and index 8 when done', now: 'Canvas <code>save()</code> and <code>restore()</code>', why: 'Leave the shared drawing state as you found it.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the write that does the work">
    <Listing
      title="ARABART.EXE · set color (CS:02F9) and paint one byte (CS:0506)"
      lines={[
        { off: '02F9', code: 'mov  dx,3CEh', note: 'Graphics Controller' },
        { off: '02FC', code: 'mov  al,1 / out dx,al', note: 'index 1: Enable Set/Reset' },
        { off: '02FF', code: 'inc dx / mov al,0Fh / out dx,al', note: 'all 4 planes take the Set/Reset color' },
        { off: '0303', code: 'dec dx / xor al,al / out dx,al', note: 'index 0: Set/Reset' },
        { off: '0307', code: 'inc dx / mov al,bl / out dx,al', note: 'the color' },
        { off: '0506', code: 'mov  ah,8', note: 'index 8: Bit Mask' },
        { off: '050B', code: 'xchg ah,al / out dx,ax', note: 'index and mask in one 16-bit OUT', hi: true },
        { off: '050E', code: 'mov  ah,es:[di]', note: 'read: loads the 4 latches' },
        { off: '0511', code: 'mov  es:[di],al', note: 'write: paints only the masked pixels', hi: true },
      ]}
      foot="The 8-instruction 'flush one byte' block appears 4 times (041D, 0443, 0506, 052C) because the handler has no subroutines."
    />
    <p><strong>Assumption:</strong> ARABART never sets the Data Rotate, Mode or Map Mask registers. It relies on BGI leaving write mode 0, "replace" and all 4 planes enabled. After a BGI call that changes these (for example XOR drawing), its colors would likely be wrong. We did not test that case.</p>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="effects">
  <h2>How do bold, italic, 3-D and outline work?</h2>
  <p class="lead">Because letters are runs, every style is a small change to the same drawing loop. Outline and drop shadow are not in ARABART; The Brave Sun builds them by calling ARABART several times.</p>
  <Figure title="فن in font 3, every style" caption="All 8 panels are drawn from his bytes with the same right edge and line bottom. Colors are the real VGA palette entries his programs use.">
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-fx-t">
      <title id="ab-fx-t">The word فن drawn plain, bold, italic, underlined, double size, in 3-D, with an outline and with a drop shadow.</title>
      {FX.map((f, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class="box-bk" x={10 + (i % 4) * 158} y={10 + Math.floor(i / 4) * 176} width="150" height="148" />
          {f.runs.map(([x, y, n, c]) => <rect fill={VGA[c]} x={13 + (i % 4) * 158 + x * 2} y={14 + Math.floor(i / 4) * 176 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
          <text x={85 + (i % 4) * 158} y={176 + Math.floor(i / 4) * 176} text-anchor="middle">{f.label}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 330 710" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-fx-tn">
      <title id="ab-fx-tn">The word فن in 8 styles.</title>
      {FX.map((f, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class="box-bk" x={6 + (i % 2) * 164} y={6 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 176} width="150" height="148" />
          {f.runs.map(([x, y, n, c]) => <rect fill={VGA[c]} x={9 + (i % 2) * 164 + x * 2} y={10 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 176 + y * 2} width={n * 2} height="2" />)}
          <text x={81 + (i % 2) * 164} y={172 + Math.floor(i / 2) * 176} text-anchor="middle">{f.label}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bold</strong> adds 1 pixel to every run and to the letter's width.</li>
    <li><strong>Italic</strong> moves each row 1 pixel further left as it goes up. The slant is measured from the line bottom, so joined letters stay joined, but 1 pixel per row is a 45° slant, which is heavy for tall fonts.</li>
    <li><strong>Size ×2</strong> doubles every skip and run, and writes each row twice.</li>
    <li><strong>3-D</strong> (function 8) draws the whole string in the shadow color, moved up and left by the font's offset, then draws it again in the main color. It resets the styles between passes, so the same <code>@ ! # $</code> marks replay the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>Outline</strong> and <strong>shadow</strong> are SUN.EXE's own C functions (04B6:01B4 and 04B6:0163): 4 copies at 1-pixel diagonals plus one on top, and a dark gray copy 6 pixels down and right.</li>
  </ul>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'Bold by widening every run by 1 pixel', now: 'Synthetic bold, when a font has no bold face', why: 'Thicken the strokes you already have.' },
      { then: 'Italic by shifting rows', now: 'Synthetic oblique (CSS <code>font-synthesis</code>)', why: 'Slant the upright glyphs instead of drawing new ones.' },
      { then: '3-D by drawing the text twice', now: 'CSS <code>text-shadow</code>', why: 'The same text, offset and colored, under the real text.' },
    ]}
  />
  <h3>Three versions of one engine</h3>
  <Figure title="PESM, ARABART 4.15, ARAB-ART 7.15" caption="Sizes are measured from the unpacked files. The order of 4.15 and 7.15 is an inference from the version numbers and from 7.15's newer font file.">
