  <p>PESM, a demo its README dates to 1991, shares 3,298 bytes with ARABART 4.15: mostly glyph tables, plus the 136-byte shaping routine, copied in as source. ARABART 4.15 turned that engine into a shared resident service. ARAB-ART 7.15 moved the fonts into a file, and the file is a byte-for-byte copy of the program's own memory from 06C3h to 55EAh: the pointers inside it are absolute addresses, and even its banner text sits at the address the code prints from. It also keeps only 43 and 44 glyphs of fonts 2 and 4, likely to save memory for SUN.</p>
</section>

<section id="quality">
  <h2>How good is the code?</h2>
  <p class="lead">It works, it is small, and three of its ideas stand out. It is also one 1,448-byte block with no subroutines, with five real bugs and some leftovers.</p>
  <Figure title="The handler, CS:003C to 05E3, and where the bugs are" caption="Box widths are not to scale; byte counts are measured. Numbers match the list below.">
    <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 170" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-q-t">
      <title id="ab-q-t">The handler split into six parts with the location of six bugs.</title>
      {MAPX.map(m => (
        <g>
          <rect class={m.name === 'shape' ? 'box-hi' : 'box'} x={m.x} y="60" width={m.w} height="96" />
          <text class:list={['t-cy', { 't-bk': m.name === 'shape' }]} x={m.x + 8} y="84">{m.off}</text>
          <text class:list={[{ 't-bk': m.name === 'shape' }]} x={m.x + 8} y="110">{m.name}</text>
          <text class:list={[{ 't-bk': m.name === 'shape' }]} x={m.x + 8} y="136">{m.size}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      {BUGS.map(b => (
        <g>
          <circle cx={MAPX[b.box].x + b.f * MAPX[b.box].w} cy="30" r="13" class="ab-bug" />
          <text class="t-bk" x={MAPX[b.box].x + b.f * MAPX[b.box].w} y="35" text-anchor="middle">{b.n}</text>
          <path class="wire-hi" d={`M${MAPX[b.box].x + b.f * MAPX[b.box].w} 43 V60`} />
        </g>
      ))}
    </svg>
    <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 300 380" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-q-tn">
      <title id="ab-q-tn">The handler's six parts, top to bottom, with bug markers.</title>
      {MAPX.map((m, i) => (
        <g>
          <rect class={m.name === 'shape' ? 'box-hi' : 'box'} x="10" y={10 + i * 60} width="280" height="52" />
          <text class:list={['t-cy', { 't-bk': m.name === 'shape' }]} x="22" y={42 + i * 60}>{m.off}</text>
          <text class:list={[{ 't-bk': m.name === 'shape' }]} x="80" y={42 + i * 60}>{m.name}, {m.size}</text>
        </g>
      ))}
      {BUGS.map((b, k) => {
        const same = BUGS.filter((o, j) => o.box === b.box && j < k).length;
        return (
          <g>
            <circle cx={214 + same * 26} cy={36 + b.box * 60} r="11" class="ab-bug" />
            <text class="t-bk" x={214 + same * 26} y={41 + b.box * 60} text-anchor="middle">{b.n}</text>
          </g>
        );
      })}
    </svg>
  </Figure>
  <ol class="ab-bugs">
    <li><strong>Bounds check uses AND, not OR</strong> (0186h): it rejects a position only if x ≥ 640 <em>and</em> y ≥ 480. In the emulator, x = 650 wrapped a letter onto the next scan line.</li>
    <li><strong>Bytes 80h and above read outside the table</strong> (0213h): he sets them to 0 but still subtracts 20h, so the lookup lands 32 bytes before the table, inside the caller's copied text. In the emulator it drew a ب from memory 86 bytes away.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure 3-D returns double the width</strong> (03BFh): it adds both passes. No known program calls it.</li>
    <li><strong>"Off screen" leaves the carry flag clear</strong> (01EFh): only a bad video mode sets it, so a caller testing carry misses the error.</li>
    <li><strong>BX is not kept</strong> (05A6h): it edits the carry bit by popping the return frame into AX, BX and DX, so callers get their own code segment back in BX.</li>
    <li><strong>Leftovers, not bugs: a <Term id="dead-store">dead store</Term></strong> (012Eh): measuring sets the cursor to (300, 0), then at once to (100, 100). Function 0 also still reports 4 fonts when there are 5.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Three ideas are better than the norm: the two "hand" bits that make shaping one short pass, the style codes placed where shaping can never produce them, and the clean hand-back of VGA state to Borland graphics. A ten-byte zero gap after his 90-byte text buffer also stops over-long strings, likely by luck rather than design.</p>
  <ThenNow
    rows={[
      { then: 'His C binding saves every register around each call', now: 'A defensive wrapper around a library with a loose contract', why: 'The caller protects itself because the callee does not.' },
      { then: 'Checking the drawing by looking at the screen', now: 'Snapshot (visual regression) tests', why: 'Both compare what was drawn with what should be drawn. Bugs 1 and 2 are the kind a fuzzer finds quickly today.' },
    ]}
  />
  <Deep title="For engineers: the fingerprint and a scored rubric">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero CALL instructions</strong> in the resident code, 72 <code>CS:</code> overrides, 10 exits that all end in <code>cli / iret</code> (the <code>cli</code> does nothing there).</li>
      <li><strong>23 padding NOPs</strong>: 19 after <code>mov byte [var],imm</code> where the variable is declared later in the source, and 4 after short forward jumps. That is the forward-reference padding of MASM or Turbo Assembler, so his data sat after his code.</li>
      <li><strong>8086 only:</strong> shifts one bit at a time, no <code>push imm</code>, no <code>enter</code>. ARABART runs on an 8088; his Borland C programs that call it need an 80186 or later.</li>
      <li><strong>Linker:</strong> the original header, kept by <Term id="diet" />, carries Borland TLINK's mark with version byte 30h.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="ab-tbl">
      <table>
        <caption>Rubric, out of 5</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Score</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>API design</td><td>4.5</td><td>One call, DOS-style registers, styles in the text, measure and 3-D built in. Weak error contract.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shaping</td><td>4.0</td><td>Correct and elegant. No automatic لا, no bidi, no vowel marks.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rendering</td><td>3.5</td><td>Correct use of Set/Reset and Bit Mask. Builds masks pixel by pixel.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data design</td><td>4.0</td><td>Table-driven, shared glyphs, metrics in spare slots. Runs larger than bitmaps.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Robustness</td><td>2.5</td><td>Bugs 1 to 5, assumed VGA state, weak install check.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Structure</td><td>2.0</td><td>No subroutines, copied blocks, dead stores, stale constants.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Overall</td><td>3.4</td><td>Original and compact; typical hygiene for a self-taught assembly programmer of 1993.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Listing
      title="ARABART.EXE · bug 1, the bounds check (CS:0186)"
      lines={[
        { off: '0186', code: 'cmp  word [x],280h', note: 'x < 640?' },
        { off: '018D', code: 'jb   019Bh', note: 'yes: go draw, y is never checked here', hi: true },
        { off: '018F', code: 'cmp  word [y],1E0h', note: 'x >= 640; is y < 480?' },
        { off: '0196', code: 'jb   019Bh', note: 'yes: still go draw', hi: true },
        { off: '0198', code: 'jmp  05A1h', note: 'only x >= 640 AND y >= 480 is refused' },
      ]}
      foot="A later check (01E4h) tests y against the real screen height, so only x can slip through. See it in the byte map at the address below."
    />
    <p><a href={at(0x186)}>Open 0186h in the byte map</a>.</p>
  </Deep>
</section>

<section id="him">
  <h2>What this tells us about him</h2>
  <p class="lead">He built the engine once, as compact table-driven <Term id="assembly" />, then reused it for at least two years: copied into PESM, served by a TSR to his C programs, and cut down for The Brave Sun.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>He designed for the people who would type.</strong> The text is the Arabic keyboard layout, the styles are the shifted digit keys, and the letter forms follow Egyptian habits (dotless final ى, Arabic-Indic digits).</li>
    <li><strong>He thought in tables.</strong> Keys to codes, codes to glyph pointers, metrics in spare slots, masks and bit orders as 8-byte tables.</li>
    <li><strong>He learned the hardware by doing.</strong> His 1991 commercial plots one pixel per BIOS call; ARABART programs the VGA Graphics Controller directly. The reversed-bit table is his own way to get x mod 8 (the code suggests trial and error, not a textbook).</li>
    <li><strong>He tested on screen, not on paper.</strong> Dead stores, copied blocks, a version constant left at 4.15 in 7.15, and a bounds check with its logic inverted likely point to code changed and checked by eye.</li>
    <li><strong>He made his own tools.</strong> The font file starts with "YASSER DOT DESIGN", and 27.9 KB of run-length glyphs were almost certainly produced by an editor or encoder he wrote. That tool is not in the archive.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

